\b ZX81 FONT FOR QL\b0\line
by Dilwyn Jones\line\line

This document describes a character set for the Sinclair QL, based on the 63-character font of the Sinclair ZX81. The font is freeware.\line\line

The Sinclair ZX81 uses a 64-character font, uppercase only, and does not conform to the ASCII standard.\line
Characters 0 to 63 represent the basic font. Codes 128 to 191 represent the same characters, but in reverse video (ink and paper colours swapped). Other character codes are used for keywords.\line\line

Using CHR$(0) to CHR$(63) directly on the QL is difficult because codes below CHR$(32) are control characters (for example CHR$(10) is linefeed). In addition, QL fonts include an extra character below the lowest code, used as a “default” character when the code is out of range. This corresponds to CHR$(-1) on the QL and may cause errors.\line
This default character is defined as a simple square to indicate an invalid ZX81 character and can be changed using any QL font editor such as FontEd.\line\line

It is therefore preferable to install the ZX81 font as an upper font (font 2), so that it appears as CHR$(128) to CHR$(191). ZX81 characters can then be printed easily using PRINT CHR$(128 + zx81_code), with reversed colours if needed.\line\line

The ZX81 font is based on an 8x8 grid. Most characters occupy the first 6 pixels horizontally, except a few such as Y and T which use 7 pixels.\line\line

On the QL, 8-pixel-wide fonts can only be used with CSIZE 1,0 (normal height) or CSIZE 1,1 (double height).\line
Since QL text lines normally start 10 pixels below the previous line, the line increment must be changed to 8 pixels to match ZX81 spacing. This can be done using the CHAR_INC command if Toolkit II is available.\line\line

\b 1. Installing the font\b0\line
The font is 587 bytes long. You must reserve this memory (for example using Toolkit II’s ALCHP function), load the font using LBYTES, then install it using CHAR_USE. Example:\line\line

\pard\li400
100 base = ALCHP(587)\line
110 LBYTES flp1_ZX81_font, base\line
120 CHAR_USE #1, 0, base\line
130 CHAR_INC #1, 8, 8\line
\pard\line

The font is now activated as the second font for window channel #1.\line
Line 100 allocates memory, line 110 loads the font, line 120 installs it (the “0” keeps the lower font unchanged), and line 130 sets character increments to 8 pixels horizontally and vertically.\line\line

You can test the font with:\line\line

\pard\li400
200 CLS\line
210 CSIZE #1, 1, 0\line
220 FOR c = 128 TO 191\line
230   PRINT CHR$(c);\line
240 END FOR c\line
\pard\line

After use, if memory was allocated with ALCHP, remember to deinstall the font and free memory:\line\line

\pard\li400
300 CHAR_USE #1, 0, 0\line
310 RECHP base\line
\pard\line

}
